<header><a href="/">MAE Academy</a><nav aria-label="Primary"><a href="/simulations">Simulations</a> <a href="/research">Research</a> <a href="/library">Library</a> <a href="/about">About</a> <a href="/contact">Contact</a></nav></header>
<main>
<h1>Control Systems</h1>
<article lang="en">
<header><h2>Root Locus</h2><p>The root locus shows how the closed-loop poles of a feedback system migrate across the complex plane as a single loop gain <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span> is swept from zero to a large value. It turns a stability question into a geometric one.</p></header>
<section><h3>Purpose and meaning</h3><p>This tool plots the locus of the roots of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1 + K\,G(s)H(s) = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> for the transfer function you enter, draws the asymptotes, breakaway points and imaginary-axis crossings, and marks the closed-loop poles that correspond to the current gain. A second plot shows the closed-loop step response for that same <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so you can watch pole position turn into damping, overshoot and speed.</p></section>
<section><h3>Mathematical model</h3>
<h4>Characteristic equation and the two criteria</h4><p>With the open-loop transfer function written as <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(s)H(s) = N(s)/D(s)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the closed-loop poles are the roots of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(s) + K\,N(s) = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A point <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math></span> lies on the locus if and only if it satisfies both the angle criterion and the magnitude criterion. The angle criterion, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum \theta_{zeros} - \sum \theta_{poles} = (2k+1)180^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, selects the geometry of the branches and does not depend on <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The magnitude criterion, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mi>K</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|K\,G(s)H(s)| = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then assigns a unique gain <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span> to every point of a branch.</p>
<h4>Branches, endpoints and asymptotes</h4><p>For <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> open-loop poles and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math></span> zeros, the locus has <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> branches. Each branch starts at a pole when <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and ends at a zero, or at infinity, when <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K \to \infty</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n - m</annotation></semantics></math></span> branches that escape follow asymptotes with real-axis centroid <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_a = (\sum p_i - \sum z_i)/(n - m)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and angles <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_a = (2k+1)\pi/(n - m)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. On the real axis, a segment belongs to the locus when the total number of real poles and zeros to its right is odd.</p>
<h4>Breakaway and break-in points</h4><p>Where two branches meet on the real axis and leave it, the gain <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(s) = -D(s)/N(s)</annotation></semantics></math></span> passes through an extremum along the axis. Solving <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dK/ds = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives candidate points, and only those whose gain <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span> is real and positive and which lie on a valid real-axis segment are true breakaway (leaving) or break-in (entering) points.</p>
<h4>Imaginary-axis crossings and stability</h4><p>The gain at which the locus enters the right half-plane follows from the Routh-Hurwitz array of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(s) + K\,N(s)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Setting the first-column entry of the <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^1</annotation></semantics></math></span> row to zero gives the critical gain <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{cr}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The auxiliary polynomial of the preceding row, evaluated at that gain, gives the crossing frequency <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{cr}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For gains below <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{cr}</annotation></semantics></math></span> all poles stay in the left half-plane and the loop is stable.</p>
<h4>Governing equations</h4>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1 + K\,G(s)H(s) = 0,\qquad K \ge 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Closed-loop characteristic equation whose roots form the locus.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mi mathvariant="normal">∠</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mi mathvariant="normal">∠</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="1em"/><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{m}\angle(s - z_j) - \sum_{i=1}^{n}\angle(s - p_i) = (2k+1)\,180^\circ,\quad k = 0, \pm 1, \pm 2, \dots</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Angle criterion: decides whether a test point belongs to the locus.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true">∣</mo><mi>K</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⟹</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo fence="true">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mo fence="true">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|K\,G(s)H(s)\right| = 1 \;\Longrightarrow\; K = \frac{\prod_{i=1}^{n}\left|s - p_i\right|}{\prod_{j=1}^{m}\left|s - z_j\right|}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Magnitude criterion: gives the gain at a point on the locus.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_a = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n} p_i - \sum_{j=1}^{m} z_j}{n - m}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Asymptote centroid on the real axis.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_a = \frac{(2k+1)\pi}{n - m},\qquad k = 0, 1, \dots, n - m - 1</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Asymptote angles for the branches that go to infinity.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⟺</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence="true">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="true">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(s) = -\frac{1}{G(s)H(s)},\qquad \frac{dK}{ds} = 0 \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \frac{d}{ds}\left[\frac{D(s)}{N(s)}\right] = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Breakaway and break-in condition from the stationary points of the gain.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⟹</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^3 + a_2 s^2 + a_1 s + K = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; K_{cr} = a_2 a_1,\qquad \omega_{cr} = \sqrt{a_1}</annotation></semantics></math></span><figcaption>Routh-Hurwitz result for a cubic: critical gain and crossing frequency.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<section><h3>Canvas parameter and control mapping</h3><dl>
<dt>Transfer function input</dt><dd>The numerator and denominator polynomials define <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(s)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(s)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Their roots are drawn as zeros and poles, and their orders fix <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the number of asymptotes <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n - m</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</dd>
<dt>Gain Calibration slider <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt><dd>Moves the closed-loop poles along the branches. The slider range is bounded by the editable limits <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{min}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{max}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which default to <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</dd>
<dt>S-plane plot</dt><dd>Shows the branches, the asymptotes with their centroid <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_a</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the current closed-loop poles. The grid can be switched between Cartesian and polar so that lines of constant <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_n</annotation></semantics></math></span> are visible.</dd>
<dt>Analytical steps panel</dt><dd>Lists the zeros, the poles, the asymptote data, the breakaway points from <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dK/ds = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the imaginary-axis crossings. Selecting a step highlights it on the plot.</dd>
<dt>Closed-loop step response</dt><dd>Simulates the step response for the current <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span> over the chosen horizon, with a default of <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mtext> s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\text{ s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, so you can relate pole damping to the response.</dd>
<dt>Transient metrics and closed-loop poles</dt><dd>Report <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_p</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_r</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_s</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the numerical pole locations that correspond to the slider setting.</dd>
</dl></section>
<section><h3>Worked numerical example</h3><h4>Root locus of a type-1 third-order loop</h4><p>Sketch the root locus for <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(s)H(s) = 1/\left(s(s+2)(s+4)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and find the asymptotes, the breakaway point and the critical gain.</p><ol>
<li><p>The loop has <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n = 3</annotation></semantics></math></span> poles at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-4</annotation></semantics></math></span> and no zeros, so <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and there are <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math></span> asymptotes. The centroid is:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_a = \frac{(0 - 2 - 4) - 0}{3 - 0} = -2</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>The asymptote angles follow from <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k = 0, 1, 2</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator="true">,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo separator="true">,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msup><mn>300</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_a = \frac{(2k+1)\pi}{3} = 60^\circ,\; 180^\circ,\; 300^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>The gain along the real axis is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(s) = -(s^3 + 6s^2 + 8s)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Setting <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dK/ds = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives a quadratic with two roots, and only the one inside the segment between <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a breakaway point:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>⇒</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mn>48</mn></msqrt></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.845</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo>−</mo><mn>3.155</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3s^2 + 12s + 8 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; s = \frac{-12 \pm \sqrt{48}}{6} = -0.845,\; -3.155</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>The root <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3.155</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s = -3.155</annotation></semantics></math></span> lies between <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-4</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where two real poles are to its right, an even count, so it is not on the locus and is rejected. The gain at the accepted point is:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence="true">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence="true">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence="true">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.845</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1.155</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>3.155</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>3.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K = \left|s\right|\left|s+2\right|\left|s+4\right| = 0.845 \times 1.155 \times 3.155 \approx 3.08</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
<li><p>The characteristic polynomial is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^3 + 6s^2 + 8s + K</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^1</annotation></semantics></math></span> row of the Routh array is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>48</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(48 - K)/6</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which vanishes at the critical gain. The auxiliary polynomial <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6s^2 + K</annotation></semantics></math></span> then gives the crossing frequency:</p><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>48</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi><msqrt><mn>8</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>j</mi><mtext> </mtext><mn>2.83</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{cr} = 6 \times 8 = 48,\qquad s = \pm j\sqrt{8} = \pm j\,2.83</annotation></semantics></math></span></li>
</ol><p>The asymptotes meet at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_a = -2</annotation></semantics></math></span> with angles <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">180^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>300</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300^\circ</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the branches break away at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.845</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s \approx -0.845</annotation></semantics></math></span> for <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>3.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K \approx 3.08</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and the loop is stable only for <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0 &lt; K &lt; 48</annotation></semantics></math></span>, crossing the imaginary axis at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>2.83</mn><mtext> rad/s</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{cr} \approx 2.83\text{ rad/s}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p></section>
<section><h3>Industrial applications and standards</h3><dl>
<dt>Robotics</dt><dd>Choosing the proportional gain of a joint position loop so that the dominant poles stay well damped and away from the imaginary axis.</dd>
<dt>Aerospace</dt><dd>Studying how an autopilot gain moves the short-period and phugoid poles, and finding the gain at which flutter-like oscillation begins.</dd>
<dt>Manufacturing</dt><dd>Tuning the loop gain of a motorised conveyor or press drive to avoid sustained oscillation when load inertia changes.</dd>
<dt>Power systems</dt><dd>Setting automatic voltage regulator gain so that generator excitation control stays stable across operating points.</dd>
</dl></section>
</article>
